Accept an already-loaded module in load_module

Spack v1.2.0 (spack/spack#51957) checks that `module load` changed
LOADEDMODULES and raises ModuleLoadError otherwise.  lmod treats loading
a module that is already loaded as a no-op (exit 0, `module is-loaded`
true), and when that module is the last one in LOADEDMODULES nothing
changes, so an external whose module the build shell had already loaded
fails to build.  Accept the load when the module is in LOADEDMODULES.

Applied by mache to its pinned Spack checkout; drop it when the pin moves
to a release that carries the upstream fix.

diff --git a/lib/spack/spack/util/module_cmd.py b/lib/spack/spack/util/module_cmd.py
index 3294b1bb05..aa4e6798bd 100644
--- a/lib/spack/spack/util/module_cmd.py
+++ b/lib/spack/spack/util/module_cmd.py
@@ -113,8 +113,12 @@ def load_module(mod):
     # Check if the module was actually loaded by comparing LOADEDMODULES
     loaded_modules_after = os.environ.get("LOADEDMODULES", "")
 
-    # If LOADEDMODULES didn't change, the module wasn't loaded
-    if loaded_modules_before == loaded_modules_after:
+    # If LOADEDMODULES didn't change, the module wasn't loaded, unless it
+    # was loaded already (loading it again is a no-op)
+    if loaded_modules_before == loaded_modules_after and not any(
+        loaded == mod or loaded.startswith(f"{mod}/")
+        for loaded in loaded_modules_after.split(os.pathsep)
+    ):
         raise ModuleLoadError(mod)
 
 
